<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<exercise xml:id="problem-derivatives-of-trigonometric-functions" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>
    Derivatives of trigonometric functions
  </title>
  
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Text before the parts.
    </p>
  </introduction>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute the limits
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } }
        </md>
        
        and
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \cos \of{ \theta } - 1 }{ \theta } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        You may wish to use the Pythagorean theorem <m> \sin^{ 2 } \of{ \theta } + \cos^{ 2 } \of{ \theta } = 1 </m>, which holds for all <m> \theta \in \R </m>, and/or the bounds
        
        <md>
          \cos \of{ \theta } \leq \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } \leq \frac{ 1 }{ \cos \of{ \theta } }
        </md>,
        
        which hold for all <m> \theta \in \R </m> so that <m> 0 \lt \abs{ \theta } \lt \frac{ \pi }{ 2 } </m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        The <xref ref="theorem-squeeze-theorem"/> implies that
        
        <md>
          1 = \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \cos \of{ \theta } } \leq \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } } \leq \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ 1 }{ \cos \of{ \theta } } } = 1
        </md>,
        
        so that 
        
        <md>
          \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } } = 1
        </md>.
        
        We now compute
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \cos \of{ \theta } - 1 }{ \theta } } \amp = \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \of{ \frac{ \cos \of{ \theta } - 1 }{ \theta } } \of{ \frac{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 }{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 } } = \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ \theta } - 1 }{ \theta \of{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 } } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ -\frac{ \sin^{ 2 } \of{ \theta } }{ \theta \of{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 } } } = \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \of{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } } \of{ -\frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \of{ \lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \theta } } } \of{ -\lim_{ \theta \to 0 }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ \theta } }{ \cos \of{ \theta } + 1 } } } = 1 \cdot 0 = 0
          </mrow>
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the trigonometric function <m> \sin \of{ x } </m> is differentiable, with derivative <m> \cos \of{ x } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        You may wish to use the sum of angles formula
        
        <md>
          \sin \of{ \alpha + \beta } = \sin \of{ \alpha } \cos \of{ \beta } + \cos \of{ \alpha } \sin \of{ \beta }
        </md>,
        
        which holds for all real numbers <m> \alpha , \beta \in \R </m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        We observe that for any point <m> c \in \R </m>,
        
        <md>
          <mrow>
            \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ x } - \sin \of{ c } }{ x - c } } \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ c + \of{ x - c } } - \sin \of{ c } }{ x - c } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \frac{ \sin \of{ c } \cos \of{ x - c } + \cos \of{ c } \sin \of{ x - c } - \sin \of{ c } }{ x - c } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \lim_{ x \to c }{ \cos \of{ c } \of{ \frac{ \sin \of{ x - c } }{ x - c } } + \sin \of{ c } \of{ \frac{ \cos \of{ x - c } - 1 }{ x - c } } }
          </mrow>
          
          <mrow>
            \amp = \cos \of{ c } \of{ 1 } + \sin \of{ c } \of{ 0 } = \cos \of{ c }
          </mrow>
        </md>,
        
        and so <m> \sin \of{ x } </m> is differentiable at <m> c </m>, with derivative <m> \cos \of{ c } </m>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the trigonometric function <m> \cos \of{ x } </m> is differentiable, with derivative <m> -\sin \of{ x } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <hint>
      <p>
        You may wish to use the trigonometric identities <m> \sin \of{ x } = \cos \of{ x - \frac{ \pi }{ 2 } } </m> and <m> \cos \of{ x } = -\sin \of{ x - \frac{ \pi }{ 2 } } </m>, which holds for all <m> x \in \R </m>.
      </p>
    </hint>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m> \cos \of{ x } = -\sin \of{ x - \frac{ \pi }{ 2 } } </m> is the composition of differentiable functions, it is itself differentiable, with derivative
        
        <md>
          -\cos \of{ x - \frac{ \pi }{ 2 } } \of{ 1 } = -\sin \of{ x }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
  
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that the trigonometric function <m> \tan \of{ x } </m> is differentiable, with derivative <m> \frac{ 1 }{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } } </m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    
    <!-- <solution>
      <p>
        Since <m> \sin \of{ x } </m> and <m> \cos \of{ x } </m> are differentiable, (c) of <xref ref="problem-the-quotient-rule"/> implies that <m> \tan \of{ x } = \frac{ \sin \of{ x } }{ \cos \of{ x } } </m> is differentiable, with derivative
        
        <md>
          \frac{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } - \sin \of{ x } \of{ -\sin \of{ x } } }{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } } = \frac{ \sin^{ 2 } \of{ x } + \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } }{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } } = \frac{ 1 }{ \cos^{ 2 } \of{ x } }
        </md>.
      </p>
    </solution> -->
  </task>
</exercise>
